1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
This is a kidney stone.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:05,200
Most are mainly calcium oxalate, which the solubility

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,440
rules call insoluble.

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:10,720
And urine always contains some calcium ions and

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,640
some oxalate ions.

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,680
Yet most of the time, no stone forms.

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,120
So "insoluble" can't be the whole story.

8
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,440
A solid only forms when there are enough

9
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:22,480
ions.

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,680
How many is enough?

11
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:29,460
Ksp describes a saturated solution, at equilibrium.

12
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:30,980
For the mixture in front of you right

13
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:34,100
now, use the same expression with the concentrations

14
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:35,940
you have at this moment.

15
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:38,620
That's the ion product, Q s p.

16
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:41,540
Just as Q is compared with K, it

17
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:44,020
tells you which way things have to go.

18
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:46,660
It uses the same powers too: for lead

19
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:50,820
iodide, Qsp is lead times iodide squared.

20
00:00:52,034 --> 00:00:55,393
If Qsp is bigger than Ksp, there are

21
00:00:55,474 --> 00:00:58,593
more ions than a saturated solution can hold.

22
00:00:58,914 --> 00:01:02,113
Ions lock together, and a precipitate forms, until

23
00:01:02,274 --> 00:01:04,593
Qsp falls back to Ksp.

24
00:01:05,234 --> 00:01:08,993
If Qsp equals Ksp, the solution is just

25
00:01:09,074 --> 00:01:10,273
saturated.

26
00:01:10,354 --> 00:01:14,273
If Qsp is smaller, nothing precipitates: the solution

27
00:01:14,354 --> 00:01:15,713
could even dissolve more.

28
00:01:16,934 --> 00:01:19,253
Here's the step most people miss.

29
00:01:19,494 --> 00:01:22,613
When two solutions are mixed, each ion spreads

30
00:01:22,734 --> 00:01:24,333
into the combined volume.

31
00:01:24,614 --> 00:01:27,973
Mix 50 millilitres with 50 millilitres, and every

32
00:01:28,054 --> 00:01:31,013
ion now has 100 millilitres to move in,

33
00:01:31,094 --> 00:01:33,573
so each concentration halves.

34
00:01:33,654 --> 00:01:37,173
Work out the concentrations after mixing first, then

35
00:01:37,254 --> 00:01:38,053
Qsp.

36
00:01:39,267 --> 00:01:39,826
Try it.

37
00:01:40,227 --> 00:01:44,466
Equal volumes of 0.010 moles per litre calcium

38
00:01:44,627 --> 00:01:49,506
chloride and 0.010 moles per litre sodium sulfate

39
00:01:49,587 --> 00:01:50,546
are mixed.

40
00:01:50,867 --> 00:01:54,066
The solid that could form is calcium sulfate:

41
00:01:54,107 --> 00:01:56,786
the rules call it slightly soluble, and its

42
00:01:56,907 --> 00:02:00,226
Ksp is 4.93 times ten to the minus

43
00:02:00,307 --> 00:02:01,066
five.

44
00:02:01,427 --> 00:02:04,546
After mixing, the volume doubles, so calcium and

45
00:02:04,627 --> 00:02:09,346
sulfate are each 0.0050 moles per litre.

46
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:16,566
Qsp is calcium times sulfate: 0.0050 times

47
00:02:16,687 --> 00:02:21,046
0.0050, which is 2.5 times ten to the

48
00:02:21,127 --> 00:02:22,006
minus five.

49
00:02:22,487 --> 00:02:26,166
That's less than Ksp, so no precipitate forms.

50
00:02:26,487 --> 00:02:27,286
Now the trap.

51
00:02:27,767 --> 00:02:33,686
Without diluting, you'd get 0.010 times 0.010,

52
00:02:33,767 --> 00:02:37,046
1.0 times ten to the minus four: four

53
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:40,486
times too big, and bigger than Ksp, so

54
00:02:40,567 --> 00:02:42,246
you'd wrongly predict a solid.

55
00:02:43,467 --> 00:02:46,586
When the volumes aren't equal, work through moles.

56
00:02:47,147 --> 00:02:51,386
25.0 millilitres of 4.0 times ten to the

57
00:02:51,547 --> 00:02:54,426
minus three moles per litre lead nitrate is

58
00:02:54,587 --> 00:02:58,906
mixed with 75.0 millilitres of 2.0 times ten

59
00:02:58,987 --> 00:03:01,386
to the minus four moles per litre sodium

60
00:03:01,467 --> 00:03:02,306
sulfate.

61
00:03:02,667 --> 00:03:06,346
Moles of lead ions are concentration times volume:

62
00:03:06,427 --> 00:03:08,906
1.0 times ten to the minus four.

63
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:14,346
Divide by the total volume, 0.100 litres:

64
00:03:14,427 --> 00:03:17,146
lead is 1.0 times ten to the minus

65
00:03:17,227 --> 00:03:18,906
three moles per litre.

66
00:03:19,227 --> 00:03:22,506
Sulfate: 1.5 times ten to the minus five

67
00:03:22,667 --> 00:03:27,226
moles, over 0.100 litres, is 1.5

68
00:03:27,387 --> 00:03:29,066
times ten to the minus four.

69
00:03:30,267 --> 00:03:34,106
Qsp for lead sulfate is 1.0 times ten

70
00:03:34,187 --> 00:03:37,306
to the minus three, times 1.5 times ten

71
00:03:37,387 --> 00:03:40,586
to the minus four: 1.5 times ten to

72
00:03:40,667 --> 00:03:41,546
the minus seven.

73
00:03:42,107 --> 00:03:46,106
The data table gives Ksp as 2.53 times

74
00:03:46,187 --> 00:03:47,866
ten to the minus eight.

75
00:03:47,947 --> 00:03:51,466
Qsp is bigger, about six times, so a

76
00:03:51,547 --> 00:03:54,106
white precipitate of lead sulfate forms.

77
00:03:55,334 --> 00:03:56,453
So here's the order.

78
00:03:56,934 --> 00:03:57,973
Dilute first.

79
00:03:58,374 --> 00:04:00,053
Then Qsp.

80
00:04:00,534 --> 00:04:02,773
Over Ksp, it falls out.

81
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,160
One more case.

82
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:08,720
Here's a saturated silver chloride solution, with solid

83
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:09,600
on the bottom.

84
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:12,160
Now add some sodium chloride.

85
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:15,360
Chloride is already part of this equilibrium, so

86
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:16,680
it's a common ion.

87
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:20,880
The chloride concentration jumps, so Qsp is suddenly

88
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:22,400
bigger than Ksp.

89
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:25,760
Silver ions and chloride ions lock onto the

90
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,760
solid until Qsp is back down to Ksp:

91
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:33,040
the equilibrium shifts left, as Le Chatelier's principle

92
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:33,680
predicts.

93
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:37,200
So less silver chloride stays dissolved.

94
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:41,360
But Ksp itself hasn't changed: only temperature changes

95
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:41,760
it.

96
00:04:42,967 --> 00:04:44,206
How much less?

97
00:04:44,487 --> 00:04:48,886
Find silver chloride's solubility in 0.10 moles per

98
00:04:48,967 --> 00:04:53,606
litre sodium chloride, with Ksp 1.77 times ten

99
00:04:53,687 --> 00:04:54,646
to the minus ten.

100
00:04:55,607 --> 00:04:57,926
In the I C E table, silver starts

101
00:04:58,007 --> 00:05:02,326
at zero, but chloride starts at 0.10.

102
00:05:02,407 --> 00:05:05,526
s dissolves, so the equilibrium row is s,

103
00:05:05,767 --> 00:05:08,166
and 0.10 plus s.

104
00:05:09,047 --> 00:05:12,486
s is tiny next to 0.10, so 0.10

105
00:05:12,647 --> 00:05:16,886
plus s is about 0.10.

106
00:05:16,967 --> 00:05:18,646
Keeping the s gives a quadratic.

107
00:05:19,047 --> 00:05:20,646
You can use it if you like, but

108
00:05:20,727 --> 00:05:23,046
HSC questions are written so you shouldn't need

109
00:05:23,127 --> 00:05:23,446
it.

110
00:05:24,667 --> 00:05:28,186
Ksp is s times 0.10.

111
00:05:28,667 --> 00:05:32,746
Divide both sides by 0.10: s is 1.77

112
00:05:32,907 --> 00:05:35,346
times ten to the minus nine.

113
00:05:35,547 --> 00:05:38,666
That's far under 5 per cent of 0.10,

114
00:05:38,747 --> 00:05:40,346
so dropping it was safe.

115
00:05:40,587 --> 00:05:42,906
In pure water, s would be the square

116
00:05:42,987 --> 00:05:46,346
root of Ksp, 1.33 times ten to the

117
00:05:46,507 --> 00:05:48,826
minus five, so it's now about seven and

118
00:05:48,907 --> 00:05:50,666
a half thousand times less.

119
00:05:51,867 --> 00:05:52,786
Your turn.

120
00:05:53,067 --> 00:05:57,226
20.0 millilitres of 1.0 times ten to the

121
00:05:57,307 --> 00:06:00,506
minus three moles per litre lead nitrate is

122
00:06:00,587 --> 00:06:04,826
mixed with 30.0 millilitres of 2.0 times ten

123
00:06:04,907 --> 00:06:07,466
to the minus three moles per litre potassium

124
00:06:07,547 --> 00:06:08,506
iodide.

125
00:06:08,827 --> 00:06:12,506
The Ksp of lead iodide is 9.8 times

126
00:06:12,587 --> 00:06:14,066
ten to the minus nine.

127
00:06:14,347 --> 00:06:15,946
Will a precipitate form?

128
00:06:16,267 --> 00:06:17,546
Pause here to answer.

129
00:06:27,967 --> 00:06:30,926
Lead is 2.0 times ten to the minus

130
00:06:31,007 --> 00:06:36,526
five moles, over the total 0.0500 litres:

131
00:06:36,607 --> 00:06:39,406
4.0 times ten to the minus four.

132
00:06:39,727 --> 00:06:42,606
Iodide is 1.2 times ten to the minus

133
00:06:42,687 --> 00:06:43,566
three.

134
00:06:43,647 --> 00:06:48,286
Qsp is lead times iodide squared: 5.8 times

135
00:06:48,367 --> 00:06:49,566
ten to the minus ten.

136
00:06:49,967 --> 00:06:53,166
That's less than Ksp, so no precipitate.

137
00:06:53,407 --> 00:06:55,566
If you forgot to square the iodide, you

138
00:06:55,687 --> 00:06:58,366
got 4.8 times ten to the minus seven,

139
00:06:58,447 --> 00:07:00,126
and wrongly predicted a solid.

140
00:07:01,334 --> 00:07:01,973
Last one.

141
00:07:02,534 --> 00:07:06,133
A beaker holds saturated calcium sulfate solution, with

142
00:07:06,214 --> 00:07:07,333
solid on the bottom.

143
00:07:07,974 --> 00:07:11,253
Solid sodium sulfate is stirred in and dissolves.

144
00:07:11,654 --> 00:07:13,333
The temperature doesn't change.

145
00:07:13,974 --> 00:07:16,213
What happens to the amount of calcium sulfate

146
00:07:16,254 --> 00:07:18,693
dissolved, and to its Ksp?

147
00:07:19,334 --> 00:07:20,773
Pause here to answer.

148
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,040
Sulfate is a common ion.

149
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:37,440
It pushes Qsp above Ksp, so calcium sulfate

150
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:40,080
precipitates, and less stays dissolved.

151
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:43,200
Ksp stays the same, because the temperature hasn't

152
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:43,840
changed.

153
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:46,960
If you said Ksp goes down, you've mixed

154
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,120
up the solubility with the constant.

155
00:07:50,334 --> 00:07:53,933
So, to predict a precipitate: find each ion's

156
00:07:54,014 --> 00:07:57,453
concentration after mixing, as moles over the total

157
00:07:57,534 --> 00:07:58,013
volume.

158
00:07:58,574 --> 00:08:02,173
Write Qsp with the same powers as Ksp,

159
00:08:02,214 --> 00:08:04,253
and compare it with Ksp from the data

160
00:08:04,334 --> 00:08:04,813
table.

161
00:08:05,294 --> 00:08:08,453
If Qsp is bigger, a precipitate forms.

162
00:08:08,814 --> 00:08:12,093
A common ion lowers solubility, but never changes

163
00:08:12,174 --> 00:08:12,973
Ksp.

164
00:08:13,374 --> 00:08:15,133
And a kidney stone can start to form

165
00:08:15,374 --> 00:08:19,653
when calcium and oxalate push Qsp past Ksp.

